Let's denote the repeating decimal $0.\overline{009}$ as $x$. That means: \begin{align}
x=0.\ (\text{repeating})_{009}009009009\ldots \nonumber
\end{align}
\begin{align}
1000x=0.\ (\text{repeating})_{009}009009009\ldots \nonumber
\end{align}
Notice the repeating part are the same with each another. Now subtract $x$ from $1000x$:
\begin{align}
1000x-x &= 009,\overline{009009009009\ldots} - 0.\overline{009009009009\ldots} \nonumber \\
1000x-x & = (009).\overline{009009009009\ldots} - 0.\overline{009009009009\ldots} \nonumber\\
1000x-x & = 9.\overline{009009009009\ldots} -0.\overline{009009009009\ldots} \nonumber \\
& = 9.\overline{009009009009\ldots} -0.\overline{009009009009\ldots} \nonumber \\
& = 9.\overline{009009009009\ldots} -0.\overline{009009009009\ldots} \nonumber \\
\end{align}
\begin{align}
9(1000x-x) = 9.\ \nonumber \\
\end{align}

<answer>Let's solve this step by step:
\begin{align}
9(1000x-x) & = 9 \nonumber \\
9000x - 9x & = 9 \nonumber \\
9000x - 9x & = 9 \nonumber \\
8991x & = 9 \nonumber \\
x & = \frac{9}{8991} \nonumber \\
& = \frac{1}{999} \nonumber
\end{align}
So, $x$ is $\frac{1}{999}$. The product of the numerator and the denominator of $\frac{1}{999}$ is $1 \times 999 = \boxed{999}$.</answer>

